{"moveId":"scriptural-awareness-does-not-solve-hiddenness","sectionId":"experience-hiddenness-and-atheism","side":"con","speaker":"Alex O'Connor","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":1605,"endEvent":1631,"startMs":3235760,"endMs":3279080,"excerpt":">> Obviously all of the the the problems that people have with religion are known to religious traditions and their scriptures. Uh at the very least sort of acknowledge them, you know, the problem of evil is acknowledged in the book of Job or in the suffering on the cross. The problem of divine hiddenness is acknowledged when Jesus' prayer goes unanswered in the Garden of Gethsemane. I mean people don't reflect on that enough. Like he he Jesus himself prays to God and and gets told no. >> Yes, which I think is quite profound. >> Well, that's an answer. No is an answer, right? >> Yeah, quite right, right. But it's like it's not, you know, it it it at the very least uh it's it's not an it's not an answer to our quandary. It's not like um you know, oh well, that's fine then. That that explains divine hiddenness. But it at least tells us that it's not like they're unaware of it, right? Um"},"proposition":"Religious scriptures acknowledge evil and unanswered prayer, but acknowledgment that God may say no does not resolve the problem of divine hiddenness.","inventoryRationale":"This directly prevents scriptural recognition from functioning as an answer to the conceded problem.","respondsToIds":["arbitrary-grace-permits-unbelief","unbelief-built-into-cosmos"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"arbitrary-grace-permits-unbelief","component":"Scripture's awareness of unanswered prayer shows recognition of the problem but supplies no explanation of the arbitrary distribution."},{"targetMoveId":"unbelief-built-into-cosmos","component":"Depicting divine refusal within scripture does not explain why a good God structures access so unevenly."}],"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"con-hiddenness-evil","tier":"central","polarity":"c