{"moveId":"atheism-hard-but-arbitrariness-serious","sectionId":"experience-hiddenness-and-atheism","side":"con","speaker":"Alex O'Connor","moveKind":"constructive","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":1733,"endEvent":1766,"startMs":3497680,"endMs":3558920,"excerpt":">> I understand and and I think that's probably true for most people, but that's one of the reasons why I think that the majority of religious thinkers in history, and I mean, anybody who's thought religiously is probably more motivated by experience than by than by argument. Argument seems to be more faith-seeking understanding than the justification for someone's belief. But yeah, I think when it comes to making the case for atheism, it's actually quite a difficult thing to do. I mean, atheism is often characterized as the rejection of a belief system, and so atheists probably spend most of their time poking holes in the fine-tuning argument and the cosmological argument and whatnot. When you have to make the positive case for there being no God, you could talk about the problem of evil. And then somebody a religious person might say, \"Well, maybe God isn't all good, you know?\" And that kind of solves the problem, at least for them. Um maybe they can't be a Christian anymore. That's a big problem for them, but like, philosophically, it's it's not that difficult of a move to make if you imagine yourself as a neutral observer. Oh, okay, I guess God's not good. All"},"proposition":"A positive case that no God exists is difficult, and evil can be accommodated by revising divine attributes, but that leaves particular religious conceptions under pressure.","inventoryRationale":"This important concession defines the con position as withholding obligation rather than asserting confident positive atheism.","respondsToIds":[],"responseComponents":[],"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"con-no-obligation","tier":"motion","polarity":"challenges"},"finalDimensions":{"logicalCoherence":{"value":83,"rationale":"Rounded mean of the t