{"moveId":"unequal-access-intensifies-hiddenness","sectionId":"practice-pluralism-and-revelation","side":"con","speaker":"Alex O'Connor","moveKind":"constructive","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":1795,"endEvent":1824,"startMs":3603400,"endMs":3657360,"excerpt":"But, I do I do think it's the the arbitrariness, I think you've put your finger on it. That's what causes people the trouble. It's not that there is suffering, it's that this person is suffering in a particular way that another person is not. It only seems to make it even worse. Like, there's only one thing worse than like freezing and starving outside on the street, and that's doing so outside of a banquet where people are inside warm and having a good time. It's that discrepancy. It's like it's like wealth inequality or something, right? It's not necessarily the problem that people are too rich or people are too poor, but it's the disparity between those two conditions. And it feels like somebody's spiritual access to the existence of God and knowledge of his sort of interaction with the world seems somewhat arbitrarily distributed amongst people. There are people who try really hard and don't get anything. There are people who don't try at all and they're sort of washed over by religious experience they weren't expecting."},"proposition":"Spiritual access is troubling because it is distributed unequally: sincere seekers may receive nothing while nonseekers unexpectedly receive powerful experiences.","inventoryRationale":"This is the clearest formulation of the con hiddenness argument from unequal access.","respondsToIds":[],"responseComponents":[],"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"con-hiddenness-evil","tier":"central","polarity":"challenges"},"finalDimensions":{"logicalCoherence":{"value":85,"rationale":"Rounded mean of the two isolated judgments; no field-level adjudication threshold was triggered.","resolution":"rounded-mean"},"evidenceWarrant":{"value":71,"rationale":"Rounded mean of the two isolated judgments; no