{"moveId":"mystical-access-is-not-the-point","sectionId":"practice-pluralism-and-revelation","side":"pro","speaker":"Ross Douthat","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":1880,"endEvent":1912,"startMs":3776800,"endMs":3848920,"excerpt":"the points you're making and say that at least in that particular form of religion, there's nothing that Jesus says that implies that people who have mystical experiences are all better than people who don't. Right? Or sort of necessarily closer to God or anything like that, right? The people who are closest to God are people who suffer and people who do good. Right? And so if you're suffering and you can't know why, right? Sometimes the Christian answer is not you know, go and you know, have a silent retreat and try and have a you know, a vision or something. It's well, you're you're suffering and your job is to treat people well. While you're suffering and maybe that treatment maybe treating people well, maybe you know, maybe you're a a leper beggar on the street and treating people well just means constantly forgiving the people who are walking by. Right? Something something like that, right? But that I I think that there is a there is a potential mistake in focusing when we're trying to resolve this question of why God shows up for some people and why he doesn't in saying the showing up is the point."},"proposition":"Unequal mystical experience need not imply unequal standing before God, because Christianity emphasizes conduct, humility, and goodness rather than privileged visions.","inventoryRationale":"This is Ross's main direct answer to unequal experiential access.","respondsToIds":["unequal-access-intensifies-hiddenness"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"unequal-access-intensifies-hiddenness","component":"Christian teaching does not treat mystical experience as spiritual rank; suffering well and treating others rightly are more central."}],"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"pro-hiddenness-freedom","tier":"subsidiary","