{"moveId":"moral-intuition-cuts-against-theism","sectionId":"freedom-suffering-and-animal-pain","side":"con","speaker":"Alex O'Connor","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":2502,"endEvent":2534,"startMs":5040600,"endMs":5103640,"excerpt":"I get what you mean. You can sort of recourse into a kind of into a kind of we can't know a kind of um uh sort of theodicy a bit a bit bit like Job's. It's like, well, who who are you? Who is this that that darkens counsel by words without knowledge? You know, who who are you to know? That's fair enough. You you can say that, but it it seems like in other contexts religious people want me to rely upon my moral intuitions. They want me to rely upon them to prove the existence of God. They say, \"Well, look, you know that certain things are right and wrong. >> Mhm. >> You know that there's a way that the world should be. And I can't prove that to you, but that intuition is so strong that you should trust it. And if that's the case, it must be grounded in God.\" And I say, \"Well, one of my intuitions is that if a single one wild animal experiences gratuitous suffering that isn't necessary, that isn't justified, if that happens on one occasion at any point in the billion-year history of life on this earth, that that at the very least counts against the plausibility of an omnibenevolent creator."},"proposition":"Religious appeals to moral intuition cannot be discarded here: one genuinely gratuitous episode of animal suffering would count against an omnibenevolent creator.","inventoryRationale":"This is the key consistency objection to answering animal pain with broad skepticism.","respondsToIds":["animal-experience-remains-uncertain"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"animal-experience-remains-uncertain","component":"The same moral intuitions used in arguments for God make even one instance of gratuitous animal suffering evidence against an omnibenevolent creator."}],"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"con-hiddenness-evil","tier"