{"moveId":"mysterious-residue-expected-under-freedom","sectionId":"freedom-suffering-and-animal-pain","side":"pro","speaker":"Ross Douthat","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":2601,"endEvent":2635,"startMs":5200880,"endMs":5269760,"excerpt":">> I I think that's I think that's certainly what you have to say if you believe in the a benevolent God. Yes. And I think that's And I think there is you do end up saying that, you know, we can account for, yeah, you know, the non-gratuity You can account for the non-gratuity of the existence of suffering, and you can account for the non-gratuity of lots of forms of suffering. But yes, there's still a mysterious residua that rests on, you know, it's the the Dostoevsky example, you know, it's like the baby's tossed on spear points, right? Or something like that. And and yes, there there again. But this is sort of also what you would to just to sort of talk my own book again, right? Not this book, but like the larger argument, right? That is what you what you would expect in a universe set up with God partially hidden, not fully hidden, but like not available at all times, right? Or available at all times, but not perceived to be available at all times, where human beings are you know, perceive themselves to be set free and to act freely, you would expect there to be some things that we can't account for."},"proposition":"A benevolent-God view must deny gratuitous suffering, but a mysterious residue is expected in a world where God is partly hidden and creatures experience freedom.","inventoryRationale":"This is Ross's qualified synthesis rather than an attempt to deny every appearance of pointless suffering.","respondsToIds":["animal-suffering-presses-triomni-god","moral-intuition-cuts-against-theism"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"animal-suffering-presses-triomni-god","component":"Many forms of suffering can be rendered non-gratuitous, while an unexplained residue is unsurprising in a partially hidden, freedo