{"moveId":"divine-love-not-oppressive-hierarchy","sectionId":"heaven-theosis-and-final-synthesis","side":"pro","speaker":"Ross Douthat","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":2954,"endEvent":2989,"startMs":5874080,"endMs":5954200,"excerpt":">> again here it's important not and and this is, you know, that this sounds like one of these things that religious people do, right? Where they're but you're not you're not supposed to see it as this kind of like the metaphor of the ladder >> Yeah, sure. >> implies like a kind that you're suffering, right? From being lower down, right? And it's more like again to take the sort of prime example. It's like God loves the Virgin Mary in a special way. He loves maybe the pregnant mother who you've done something terrible to and who achieves, you know, who has some kind of experience because of it. He loves them in a in a special way that doesn't reduce the experience of being loved by God that that unborn child feels or that you would feel if if you yourself repent, right? Um and again, I don't, you know, this is not like this is not a a provable Yeah. A provable thing, but it's I I don't think it's impossible to conceptualize, right? But like like like the, you know, the the basic claim, right? Again, is that sort of God's love is like human love, but different from human love, above human love and so on and I don't I don't think it's ridiculous to say that like in the afterlife you could be content with God's love"},"proposition":"Differences in heavenly fulfillment need not be oppressive ranks, because every person can be fully content in divine love even when others receive it differently.","inventoryRationale":"This is Ross's direct attempt to remove the alleged harm from unequal heavenly outcomes.","respondsToIds":["human-violence-fixes-eternal-rank"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"human-violence-fixes-eternal-rank","component":"Different modes of divine love need not produce deprivation, resentment, oppression,