{"schemaVersion":"1.2-standalone-score-blind-inventory","protocolId":"assessment-production-standalone-debate-v1","status":"complete-and-frozen","debateNumber":"217","debateId":"douthat-oconnor-religious-belief-obligation-2026","assessmentModel":"5.6 Sol","motion":"Does the available evidence make religious belief an obligation?","isolation":{"legacyAssessmentsUnavailable":true,"calculatedTotalsUnavailable":true,"winnerLabelsUnavailable":true,"publicationProseUnavailable":true,"otherDebatesUnavailable":true},"routes":[{"side":"pro","speaker":"Ross Douthat","position":"Religious belief as rational obligation","bridges":[{"bridgeId":"pro-obligation","tier":"motion","statement":"The available evidence makes serious religious belief or practice an intellectual obligation."},{"bridgeId":"pro-cumulative-evidence","tier":"central","statement":"Converging evidence from consciousness, cosmic order, and experience favors a religious universe."},{"bridgeId":"pro-practical-religion","tier":"central","statement":"Religious practice is warranted before every theological uncertainty or doctrinal difference is resolved."},{"bridgeId":"pro-hiddenness-freedom","tier":"subsidiary","statement":"Divine hiddenness and uneven revelation can serve freedom, moral action, and a meaningful human story."},{"bridgeId":"pro-suffering-theosis","tier":"subsidiary","statement":"Suffering can be compatible with divine goodness when creaturely development and ultimate fulfillment are considered."}]},{"side":"con","speaker":"Alex O'Connor","position":"Skeptical evidential challenge","bridges":[{"bridgeId":"con-no-obligation","tier":"motion","statement":"The evidence remains too uncertain and underdetermined to make religious belief obligatory."},{"bridgeId":"con-underdetermination","tier":"central","statement":"Consciousness and cosmic order do not uniquely support theism or a personal creator."},{"bridgeId":"con-hiddenness-evil","tier":"central","statement":"Arbitrary hiddenness and apparently gratuitous suffering weigh against the relevant religious conceptions of God."},{"bridgeId":"con-pluralism-doctrine","tier":"subsidiary","statement":"Religious plurality makes premature practice liable to produce materially false conceptions of God."},{"bridgeId":"con-freedom-heaven","tier":"subsidiary","statement":"Appeals to freedom, earthly formation, and heavenly fulfillment generate unresolved internal tensions."}]}],"sections":[{"sectionId":"obligation-and-consciousness","title":"Obligation and the evidence from consciousness","weightPercent":15},{"sectionId":"fine-tuning-and-cosmic-order","title":"Fine-tuning and cosmic order","weightPercent":18},{"sectionId":"experience-hiddenness-and-atheism","title":"Experience, hiddenness, and the atheist alternative","weightPercent":14},{"sectionId":"practice-pluralism-and-revelation","title":"Practice, pluralism, and uneven revelation","weightPercent":17},{"sectionId":"freedom-suffering-and-animal-pain","title":"Freedom, suffering, and animal pain","weightPercent":20},{"sectionId":"heaven-theosis-and-final-synthesis","title":"Heaven, theosis, and final synthesis","weightPercent":16}],"moves":[{"moveId":"religious-interest-obligatory","sectionId":"obligation-and-consciousness","side":"pro","speaker":"Ross Douthat","moveKind":"constructive","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":10,"endEvent":24,"startMs":17360,"endMs":61400,"excerpt":">> It means that there are enough reasons sort of in front of us that don't require insanely complicated philosophical arguments or personal mystical experiences to think that the universe exists for a reason, that it is a fashioned or created thing rather than a accidental or entirely contingent thing, and that human life has some sort of cosmic purpose within that framework. And I take that to be sort of the jumping-off point for religious exploration, interest, and belief."},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"The opening welcome identifies Ross, and the first answer follows the host's explicit question.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"pro-obligation","tier":"motion","polarity":"supports"},"respondsToIds":[],"responseComponents":[],"proposition":"Accessible reasons indicate that the universe is created for a reason and human life has cosmic purpose, making religious exploration and belief obligatory.","inventoryRationale":"This is the opening formulation of the motion-side burden and defines the claimed obligation."},{"moveId":"consciousness-exceeds-material-description","sectionId":"obligation-and-consciousness","side":"pro","speaker":"Ross Douthat","moveKind":"constructive","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":156,"endEvent":181,"startMs":317320,"endMs":381680,"excerpt":"you know, there's a form of supernatural experience this It's easy to miss it because it's right here in front of our eyes. Like you and I are in a particular way having supernatural experiences already, right now, just in communicating with one another. We are sort of sharing thoughts, hopefully, you know, depending on the how the conversation goes, uh that are very very difficult to reduce to physical descriptions of sound waves traveling from my mouth to your ears and back and forth, very difficult to reduce to brain mapping and neurons firing. Even if you can sort of reduce them to localities and say, \"Oh, this part of the conversation activates that part of Alex's brain.\" And so on. There is this really profound as you yourself, I think, would agree, difficult to bridge philosophical chasm between the materialist description and what seems to happen to human beings in conscious experience."},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"A marked answer continues Ross's extended response about why consciousness points beyond materialism.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"pro-cumulative-evidence","tier":"central","polarity":"supports"},"respondsToIds":[],"responseComponents":[],"proposition":"Ordinary conscious communication cannot be reduced to sound waves or neural locations, revealing a gap between material description and conscious experience.","inventoryRationale":"This is the foundational consciousness argument supporting a reality not exhausted by matter."},{"moveId":"panpsychism-points-religiously","sectionId":"obligation-and-consciousness","side":"pro","speaker":"Ross Douthat","moveKind":"constructive","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":225,"endEvent":249,"startMs":465840,"endMs":521479,"excerpt":"I think if you make the panpsychist move and say that consciousness is sort of a widespread property, that already takes you in a religious direction because then it becomes very unlikely that you and I are instantiation of consciousness is the highest and most powerful and most important in the universe. Not Not impossible, right? But But it seems it seems a bit unlikely. So, even I would say it's a very swift movement from panpsychism to pantheism, right? And then you're already in the terrain not of Western Christianity, but of, you know, certain Eastern religions and so on, right? And you could get from there to Christianity through various modifications. You could be a panentheist and then the next thing you know, you're, you know, you're saying the Hail Mary or something, right? So, that's one movement you could make."},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Ross continues the same answer and explicitly develops the panpsychist route introduced moments earlier.","importance":2,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"pro-cumulative-evidence","tier":"central","polarity":"supports"},"respondsToIds":[],"responseComponents":[],"proposition":"If consciousness is widespread, human consciousness is unlikely to be its highest form, so panpsychism quickly opens onto pantheistic or other religious possibilities.","inventoryRationale":"This supplies a distinct inference from foundational consciousness to a religious worldview."},{"moveId":"mind-combination-underdetermines-god","sectionId":"obligation-and-consciousness","side":"con","speaker":"Alex O'Connor","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":419,"endEvent":446,"startMs":858640,"endMs":910360,"excerpt":"brain. But, I think that when you then make the move of saying something like you know, look uh it'd be really unusual if our brains were the most complex version of that. And I think maybe, but I'm not sure. I'm not I'm not sure if that's the case. It depends on how you think minds combine because there's some reason why I have a mind and you have a mind and they seem to be separate from each other. And if God has a mind which is bigger and more complicated and sort of the pinnacle version of this, I kind of want to ask what it might be made out of in the same way. Like if you say something like if you take all of the conscious stuff of the universe and add it all up together, that is the mind of God, it kind of sounds like you're a pantheist and saying that the universe is just God's mind, which is cool. That's great. But, it doesn't really align very well with Christianity in my view."},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"The host's long response follows Ross's answer and uses first-person distinctions between Alex's and Ross's views.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"con-underdetermination","tier":"central","polarity":"challenges"},"respondsToIds":["panpsychism-points-religiously"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"panpsychism-points-religiously","component":"Foundational consciousness leaves the combination problem unresolved and may yield pantheism rather than a distinct divine mind."}],"proposition":"Even if consciousness is foundational, separate minds raise a combination problem, and aggregating consciousness suggests pantheism rather than Christianity's distinct creator.","inventoryRationale":"This is the first substantial skeptical rebuttal to the proposed inference from consciousness to God."},{"moveId":"pantheism-opposes-classical-creator","sectionId":"obligation-and-consciousness","side":"con","speaker":"Alex O'Connor","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":515,"endEvent":552,"startMs":1063320,"endMs":1141680,"excerpt":">> Mhm. Mhm. I suppose I I I get a bit nervous about lumping together pantheism or classical theism. When to me these are I I don't see, in other words, pantheism as a step in the direction towards classical theism and perhaps I I think the way that you might because I think that they're in many ways anathema to each other. I think classical theism requires this separation between the creature and the creation. God is a sort of unique sui generis thing which is the wellspring creator of this world which is somehow sort of separate from him. Um whereas I think that these theories of consciousness that are increasing in popularity like panpsychism and idealism don't so much sort of posit this great big mind to explain everything we see but explain everything we see as a great big mind or as lots of sort of trillions and trillions of tiny little minds. And I feel like if you follow that that line it might actually kind of lead you away from this idea of a sort of separable creator who sits outside of the universe. If the reason we're supposed to sort of get there in the first place is because we've noticed that the universe seems to be filled with mind. You know what I mean? It it seems to collapse this creature-creation distinction a bit."},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"A marked host turn develops Alex's concern about treating pantheism as a step toward classical theism.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"con-underdetermination","tier":"central","polarity":"challenges"},"respondsToIds":["panpsychism-points-religiously"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"panpsychism-points-religiously","component":"Panpsychism and idealism collapse the creator-creation distinction instead of supporting a separate classical-theist creator."}],"proposition":"Pantheism and classical theism may be opposed: mind-filled or idealist theories explain the universe as mind and can erase the creator-creation distinction.","inventoryRationale":"This sharpens the central underdetermination objection by contrasting the resulting metaphysical options."},{"moveId":"practice-before-complete-theology","sectionId":"obligation-and-consciousness","side":"pro","speaker":"Ross Douthat","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":603,"endEvent":623,"startMs":1246880,"endMs":1291600,"excerpt":"astray? Sure, but the first step of saying you know, there's probably a higher power and this higher power is probably interested in what human beings are doing and the way they live their lives and the ancient religious tradition closest to me probably offers some access to that higher power that I shouldn't just dismiss. I think that's a very important step and you don't want to Yeah, you don't I you don't want to just hold back and say well, until I've settled completely in my mind what God means, I can't legitimately pray to God. I think that's I I guess I think that's the that's the mistake."},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Ross's marked response directly addresses Alex's distinction between pantheism and classical theism.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"pro-practical-religion","tier":"central","polarity":"supports"},"respondsToIds":["pantheism-opposes-classical-creator"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"pantheism-opposes-classical-creator","component":"Important theological differences need not be completely settled before prayer or participation in an inherited religious tradition is warranted."}],"proposition":"Uncertainty between pantheism and classical theism should not suspend practice, because a nearby tradition may still mediate a real higher power concerned with human life.","inventoryRationale":"This establishes the practical bridge from generic religious evidence to action despite doctrinal uncertainty."},{"moveId":"fine-tuning-favors-theism","sectionId":"fine-tuning-and-cosmic-order","side":"pro","speaker":"Ross Douthat","moveKind":"constructive","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":710,"endEvent":748,"startMs":1461240,"endMs":1547200,"excerpt":">> I wouldn't say that I'm impressed with it as something certainly that would it seems much more likely >> Mhm. >> Sure. >> under theistic conditions than under non-theistic conditions. And if you again imagine yourself rewinding the clock to debates between atheists and believers in the Enlightenment era, you know, if it's Hume and Voltaire or it's the late 19th century and you know, whichever time period you pick. Take that landscape and introduce into it the current scientific understanding of the likelihood of a universe like ours existing, which is, you know, on the order not of like, oh well, for every thousand universes you get one that might have life. It's more like on the order of for every quadrillion quadrillion potential universes you get you get one like this. I guess my view is if you introduce that point to any prior era of arguments between the religious and the non-religious, it would be immediately recognized as you know, better better for the theists than the non-theists >> Yeah, I mean >> in a pretty in a pretty significant way and not what would be expected on atheistic premises. Doesn't mean that"},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"A marked Ross answer responds to Alex's explicit question about fine-tuning and creation.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"pro-cumulative-evidence","tier":"central","polarity":"supports"},"respondsToIds":[],"responseComponents":[],"proposition":"A life-permitting universe appears vastly more likely under theism than non-theism, even if fine-tuning is not a conclusive proof.","inventoryRationale":"This is the principal pro inference from physical fine-tuning to a designed cosmos."},{"moveId":"converging-lines-support-religion","sectionId":"fine-tuning-and-cosmic-order","side":"pro","speaker":"Ross Douthat","moveKind":"constructive","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":755,"endEvent":780,"startMs":1557080,"endMs":1615000,"excerpt":"I don't think there is one single slam dunk argument for religion. I I do think that the intellectual case for being religious lies in convergence of these different lines, right? Where you say okay, the you know, the line of evidence from fine-tuning seems to be more what you'd think you'd get in a designed cosmos. Likewise, the lines of evidence from consciousness. Likewise, the lines of evidence from religious and mystical experience. And when you've got enough converging lines, you say, well, the non-theist needs basically an explanation over here to take care of this one, an explanation over here to take care of this one. At some point you just say, okay, but this broadly speaking, this looks like a universe that a mind created and is interested in you know, things we're doing and communicating with us. That's that's sort of That's"},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Ross continues uninterrupted and explicitly states his broader cumulative method.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"pro-cumulative-evidence","tier":"central","polarity":"supports"},"respondsToIds":[],"responseComponents":[],"proposition":"Fine-tuning, consciousness, and religious experience jointly form a cumulative case that is simpler than separate non-theistic explanations for every line of evidence.","inventoryRationale":"This is the debate's main synthesis of the pro evidential route rather than a single-argument claim."},{"moveId":"fine-tuning-needs-distribution","sectionId":"fine-tuning-and-cosmic-order","side":"con","speaker":"Alex O'Connor","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":901,"endEvent":935,"startMs":1820680,"endMs":1886800,"excerpt":"And so much rides on our analysis of the potential possible ranges. This isn't some like observed value that we that we sort of see in the universe. It's the result of a calculation from quantum mechanics which we know is wrong because the figure it gives us is way too strong. And so I feel as though so much rides on knowing exactly what the possible sort of different options were and also knowing what the probability distribution is. The analogy that I've sometimes given is this like if I if I were to roll a a die and it came up four, what were the odds of that? You might be tempted to say about one in six. But I didn't tell you how many sides the die had. Okay, so maybe it's >> Dungeons We're playing Dungeons and Dragons, right? >> what it's got it's got 12, right? Okay, so what are the odds of getting four? Well, one in 12. Not quite because I didn't tell you what numbers were on each side of the die. Okay, it's a normal 12-sided die and still might not be one in 12 cuz you don't know if the die is weighted. And you need to know all of those facts in order to to understand the probability."},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Alex's marked response continues his detailed explanation of uncertainty in fine-tuning probabilities.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"con-underdetermination","tier":"central","polarity":"challenges"},"respondsToIds":["fine-tuning-favors-theism"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"fine-tuning-favors-theism","component":"A small life-permitting range establishes no probability without justified possible values and a probability distribution over them."}],"proposition":"Fine-tuning probabilities depend on unknown possible ranges and distributions, just as a die result cannot be evaluated without its sides, labels, and weighting.","inventoryRationale":"This gives the load-bearing technical reason for withholding the claimed probabilistic inference."},{"moveId":"unknown-variables-limit-confidence","sectionId":"fine-tuning-and-cosmic-order","side":"con","speaker":"Alex O'Connor","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":991,"endEvent":1006,"startMs":1978120,"endMs":2007200,"excerpt":"Now, that doesn't undo the fine-tuning argument, but I think it at least sort of puts a dent in the confidence with which many people often present it. You could I think you could change the the strength of the strong nuclear force by like 50% 50% and everything would be fine. It's just that when you consider how big it could possibly be in principle, it's like a tiny tiny amount of a variance. But we just I just feel like we don't know enough about the about the variables, you know?"},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Alex explicitly draws the conclusion of his uninterrupted fine-tuning analysis.","importance":2,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"con-underdetermination","tier":"central","polarity":"challenges"},"respondsToIds":["fine-tuning-favors-theism"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"fine-tuning-favors-theism","component":"Uncertainty about the permissible ranges and variables reduces warranted confidence even if it does not refute fine-tuning outright."}],"proposition":"The probability problem does not defeat fine-tuning outright, but unknown variables and broad life-permitting intervals substantially reduce confidence in it.","inventoryRationale":"This is Alex's important qualified conclusion rather than a mere repetition of the preceding analogy."},{"moveId":"scientific-reaction-supports-significance","sectionId":"fine-tuning-and-cosmic-order","side":"pro","speaker":"Ross Douthat","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":1023,"endEvent":1056,"startMs":2037480,"endMs":2108680,"excerpt":"To me, what is notable is you approach these things as a layman and you try and look at what are the scientists actually impressed by, >> Yes, right. Sure, sure, sure. >> and generally my sense is that in fact, scientists and philosophers of science have ended up quite impressed by this argument. Now, maybe to a fault, right? But that this is why you have this sort of It's not the only reason, but it's it's why you have um among people analyzing and making arguments around this issue who are not religious believers, which is of course a great many people, sort of the turn to conceptions of the multiverse, assumptions about sort of a near infinity or infinity of worlds, and so on. Um, and in that sense, you know, that the fact of that turn, which is again quite different, I think, from a lot of perspectives that were pretty confidently held, let's say, in the late 1800s or early 20th century. The fact of that turn suggests that there is, in fact, something quite striking here. It also, though, carries"},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Ross's marked response follows Alex's probability critique and appeals to scientific reaction to fine-tuning.","importance":2,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"pro-cumulative-evidence","tier":"central","polarity":"supports"},"respondsToIds":["fine-tuning-needs-distribution","unknown-variables-limit-confidence"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"fine-tuning-needs-distribution","component":"Even allowing technical uncertainty, scientists' attention and multiverse proposals indicate that the observed order is genuinely striking."},{"targetMoveId":"unknown-variables-limit-confidence","component":"Possible revisions to numerical details do not remove the evidential significance reflected in nonreligious scientific responses."}],"proposition":"Although particular calculations may change, scientists' concern with fine-tuning and recourse to multiverse explanations show that the phenomenon remains striking.","inventoryRationale":"This is Ross's principal answer to the technical uncertainty challenge."},{"moveId":"logical-range-defeats-precise-odds","sectionId":"fine-tuning-and-cosmic-order","side":"con","speaker":"Alex O'Connor","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":1216,"endEvent":1243,"startMs":2426920,"endMs":2481640,"excerpt":"only non-arbitrary and certain way that we can we can say we know what the sort of possible limits are to think what's logically possible. Like what would just is logically non-contradictory to to imagine the constant being. And it seems like it becomes like infinite in scope. It could be literally anything. It could have any strength. It could have 10 100 a billion a trillion. It could be anything. There's no upper bound. At which point you make the sort of total bound infinity and make it impossible to meaningfully talk about probabilities anymore. I guess one in infinity does sound more impressive. >> It does. No, >> to put the precise numbers that are often so impressive. You hear sort of one in 10 to the 120th power and stuff. And I think a lot of that is is perhaps I want to say more dubious, but you know, we don't know for sure. But at the very least I think the only non-arbitrary way to do it would be be to make it infinite. And that makes it maybe a bit more meaningless, you know? Um, I don't"},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Alex resumes after a brief exchange and states a distinct probability objection in his own extended turn.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"con-underdetermination","tier":"central","polarity":"challenges"},"respondsToIds":["fine-tuning-favors-theism","scientific-reaction-supports-significance"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"fine-tuning-favors-theism","component":"The only non-arbitrary logical range may be unbounded, making the familiar precise fine-tuning odds meaningless."},{"targetMoveId":"scientific-reaction-supports-significance","component":"Scientific interest does not itself supply a justified upper bound or probability measure for the constants."}],"proposition":"If a constant's non-arbitrary possible range is the full logically possible and unbounded range, familiar precise fine-tuning probabilities lose meaningful foundations.","inventoryRationale":"This is a distinct and more fundamental version of the probability-distribution challenge."},{"moveId":"order-remains-explanatory-mystery","sectionId":"fine-tuning-and-cosmic-order","side":"pro","speaker":"Ross Douthat","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":1244,"endEvent":1271,"startMs":2479400,"endMs":2539120,"excerpt":">> Yeah, I think there's a way in which those kind of attempts to do sharp numbers >> Mhm. >> are just sort of pulling us back towards a original original version of the religious argument, which is to say like you're not going to know the exact probabilities, but >> you know, why why is there something rather than nothing is already it's already a challenging question. And why is the something so ordered and law-bound and predictable and mathematically beautiful? Why does mathematics work? All you know, all of these these kind of arguments that predate fine-tuning. >> Yeah. >> And I think they're themselves quite good arguments, right? Um, I don't think that they're actually undone by Darwinism. As much power as Darwinism has to explain certain features of reality."},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Ross's marked turn directly reframes Alex's objection away from exact numerical probabilities.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"pro-cumulative-evidence","tier":"central","polarity":"supports"},"respondsToIds":["fine-tuning-needs-distribution","logical-range-defeats-precise-odds"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"fine-tuning-needs-distribution","component":"Exact odds are unnecessary for the older explanatory question of why anything orderly and mathematically intelligible exists."},{"targetMoveId":"logical-range-defeats-precise-odds","component":"Failure to establish sharp numerical bounds leaves intact the broader argument from law-bound and intelligible order."}],"proposition":"Even without exact probabilities, the existence of something law-bound, predictable, and mathematically beautiful remains a strong design-oriented explanatory mystery.","inventoryRationale":"This is the pro fallback from contested fine-tuning numbers to the broader argument from cosmic order."},{"moveId":"arbitrary-grace-permits-unbelief","sectionId":"experience-hiddenness-and-atheism","side":"pro","speaker":"Ross Douthat","moveKind":"constructive","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":1519,"endEvent":1549,"startMs":3052560,"endMs":3114760,"excerpt":"I mean, certainly one of the most powerful ones is just like the difficulty in figuring out what God is up to >> Mhm. >> in the ways that different human beings uh, experience, you know, experience human life. >> Yeah, yeah. >> Right? Where, you know, if you're a Catholic, you think that there's a lot of miracles out there. You have a lot of lot of excellent miracle stories in Catholicism. Some of them have, you know, reached the level of being endlessly debated by the rationalist community on the internet. But, everyone doesn't get a miracle, right? Lots of lots of people don't get don't get miracles. People who people, you know, can one person can get this incredibly intense experience where you listen to them and you're like, \"Of course you believe in God, right? Of course you have absolute confidence in in Jesus or the Virgin Mary or anything else.\" Um, but that is denied to the next five people in line for reasons that are certainly beyond my capacity or knowledge. Now,"},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Ross answers Alex's question about the strongest considerations against religious belief.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"pro-hiddenness-freedom","tier":"subsidiary","polarity":"supports"},"respondsToIds":[],"responseComponents":[],"proposition":"The strongest challenge is the unexplained unevenness of miracles and religious experience, since profound access is granted to some people but denied to others.","inventoryRationale":"This is a major pro concession identifying the most serious evidential burden on religion."},{"moveId":"unbelief-built-into-cosmos","sectionId":"experience-hiddenness-and-atheism","side":"pro","speaker":"Ross Douthat","moveKind":"constructive","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":1575,"endEvent":1604,"startMs":3169360,"endMs":3238120,"excerpt":"So, there's all kinds of ways, all kinds of people, all kinds of contexts where people have them and all kinds of contexts where people don't. And it's just I think understandable for the non-believer to say, well, this all seems ridiculously arbitrary. And and we and and the religious response has to be, well, you know, you sort of assume that that there's there's an actual plan here and these these things connect to things that God is doing and is interested in doing in different people's lives. But there's no definite answer to why one person gets the profound thing and another person doesn't. And then what comes out of that is just a sense that as much as I may push you or anyone else to take religion seriously or become religious, clearly the cosmos is set up to allow for unbelief. Right? Like whatever God's up to, it includes the possibility, the allowance, that you may never, you know, that that you may not be a religious believer, right? And that's that's sort of that seems built in."},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Ross continues the same answer and draws an explicit conclusion from the uneven distribution of experience.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"pro-hiddenness-freedom","tier":"subsidiary","polarity":"supports"},"respondsToIds":[],"responseComponents":[],"proposition":"Because sincere seeking does not reliably produce experience, the cosmos appears deliberately structured to allow the genuine possibility of unbelief.","inventoryRationale":"This converts the hiddenness concession into a central feature that the pro account must explain."},{"moveId":"scriptural-awareness-does-not-solve-hiddenness","sectionId":"experience-hiddenness-and-atheism","side":"con","speaker":"Alex O'Connor","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":1605,"endEvent":1631,"startMs":3235760,"endMs":3279080,"excerpt":">> Obviously all of the the the problems that people have with religion are known to religious traditions and their scriptures. Uh at the very least sort of acknowledge them, you know, the problem of evil is acknowledged in the book of Job or in the suffering on the cross. The problem of divine hiddenness is acknowledged when Jesus' prayer goes unanswered in the Garden of Gethsemane. I mean people don't reflect on that enough. Like he he Jesus himself prays to God and and gets told no. >> Yes, which I think is quite profound. >> Well, that's an answer. No is an answer, right? >> Yeah, quite right, right. But it's like it's not, you know, it it it at the very least uh it's it's not an it's not an answer to our quandary. It's not like um you know, oh well, that's fine then. That that explains divine hiddenness. But it at least tells us that it's not like they're unaware of it, right? Um"},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"The marked host turn cites scripture and then distinguishes acknowledgment from explanation.","importance":2,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"con-hiddenness-evil","tier":"central","polarity":"challenges"},"respondsToIds":["arbitrary-grace-permits-unbelief","unbelief-built-into-cosmos"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"arbitrary-grace-permits-unbelief","component":"Scripture's awareness of unanswered prayer shows recognition of the problem but supplies no explanation of the arbitrary distribution."},{"targetMoveId":"unbelief-built-into-cosmos","component":"Depicting divine refusal within scripture does not explain why a good God structures access so unevenly."}],"proposition":"Religious scriptures acknowledge evil and unanswered prayer, but acknowledgment that God may say no does not resolve the problem of divine hiddenness.","inventoryRationale":"This directly prevents scriptural recognition from functioning as an answer to the conceded problem."},{"moveId":"atheism-hard-but-arbitrariness-serious","sectionId":"experience-hiddenness-and-atheism","side":"con","speaker":"Alex O'Connor","moveKind":"constructive","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":1733,"endEvent":1766,"startMs":3497680,"endMs":3558920,"excerpt":">> I understand and and I think that's probably true for most people, but that's one of the reasons why I think that the majority of religious thinkers in history, and I mean, anybody who's thought religiously is probably more motivated by experience than by than by argument. Argument seems to be more faith-seeking understanding than the justification for someone's belief. But yeah, I think when it comes to making the case for atheism, it's actually quite a difficult thing to do. I mean, atheism is often characterized as the rejection of a belief system, and so atheists probably spend most of their time poking holes in the fine-tuning argument and the cosmological argument and whatnot. When you have to make the positive case for there being no God, you could talk about the problem of evil. And then somebody a religious person might say, \"Well, maybe God isn't all good, you know?\" And that kind of solves the problem, at least for them. Um maybe they can't be a Christian anymore. That's a big problem for them, but like, philosophically, it's it's not that difficult of a move to make if you imagine yourself as a neutral observer. Oh, okay, I guess God's not good. All"},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Alex's marked turn gives his own synthesis after the exchange about suffering and Christian symbolism.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"con-no-obligation","tier":"motion","polarity":"challenges"},"respondsToIds":[],"responseComponents":[],"proposition":"A positive case that no God exists is difficult, and evil can be accommodated by revising divine attributes, but that leaves particular religious conceptions under pressure.","inventoryRationale":"This important concession defines the con position as withholding obligation rather than asserting confident positive atheism."},{"moveId":"unequal-access-intensifies-hiddenness","sectionId":"practice-pluralism-and-revelation","side":"con","speaker":"Alex O'Connor","moveKind":"constructive","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":1795,"endEvent":1824,"startMs":3603400,"endMs":3657360,"excerpt":"But, I do I do think it's the the arbitrariness, I think you've put your finger on it. That's what causes people the trouble. It's not that there is suffering, it's that this person is suffering in a particular way that another person is not. It only seems to make it even worse. Like, there's only one thing worse than like freezing and starving outside on the street, and that's doing so outside of a banquet where people are inside warm and having a good time. It's that discrepancy. It's like it's like wealth inequality or something, right? It's not necessarily the problem that people are too rich or people are too poor, but it's the disparity between those two conditions. And it feels like somebody's spiritual access to the existence of God and knowledge of his sort of interaction with the world seems somewhat arbitrarily distributed amongst people. There are people who try really hard and don't get anything. There are people who don't try at all and they're sort of washed over by religious experience they weren't expecting."},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Alex continues his marked turn and explicitly identifies arbitrary disparity as the central problem.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"con-hiddenness-evil","tier":"central","polarity":"challenges"},"respondsToIds":[],"responseComponents":[],"proposition":"Spiritual access is troubling because it is distributed unequally: sincere seekers may receive nothing while nonseekers unexpectedly receive powerful experiences.","inventoryRationale":"This is the clearest formulation of the con hiddenness argument from unequal access."},{"moveId":"mystical-access-is-not-the-point","sectionId":"practice-pluralism-and-revelation","side":"pro","speaker":"Ross Douthat","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":1880,"endEvent":1912,"startMs":3776800,"endMs":3848920,"excerpt":"the points you're making and say that at least in that particular form of religion, there's nothing that Jesus says that implies that people who have mystical experiences are all better than people who don't. Right? Or sort of necessarily closer to God or anything like that, right? The people who are closest to God are people who suffer and people who do good. Right? And so if you're suffering and you can't know why, right? Sometimes the Christian answer is not you know, go and you know, have a silent retreat and try and have a you know, a vision or something. It's well, you're you're suffering and your job is to treat people well. While you're suffering and maybe that treatment maybe treating people well, maybe you know, maybe you're a a leper beggar on the street and treating people well just means constantly forgiving the people who are walking by. Right? Something something like that, right? But that I I think that there is a there is a potential mistake in focusing when we're trying to resolve this question of why God shows up for some people and why he doesn't in saying the showing up is the point."},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Ross's marked response invokes Jesus's teaching to answer Alex's focus on unequal mystical access.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"pro-hiddenness-freedom","tier":"subsidiary","polarity":"supports"},"respondsToIds":["unequal-access-intensifies-hiddenness"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"unequal-access-intensifies-hiddenness","component":"Christian teaching does not treat mystical experience as spiritual rank; suffering well and treating others rightly are more central."}],"proposition":"Unequal mystical experience need not imply unequal standing before God, because Christianity emphasizes conduct, humility, and goodness rather than privileged visions.","inventoryRationale":"This is Ross's main direct answer to unequal experiential access."},{"moveId":"hidden-person-called-to-virtue","sectionId":"practice-pluralism-and-revelation","side":"pro","speaker":"Ross Douthat","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":1959,"endEvent":1976,"startMs":3942680,"endMs":3988000,"excerpt":"I'm supposed to, you know, help people, right? Um and, you know, the the the parable of the sheep and the goats, right? The the goats show up and the sheep show up and they are praised for for, you know, helping the poor and visiting the sick and all the rest. Anyway, you you you know you know the story, right? But, this is this is one answer, at least, right, to that problem, that the solution to the problem of not knowing why God hasn't appeared to you and given you a great vision is that when God appeared on Earth, he said that, you know, you were supposed to help people and be good in a pretty profound and extreme way."},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Ross concludes the same answer by stating what a person lacking revelation should do.","importance":2,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"pro-practical-religion","tier":"central","polarity":"supports"},"respondsToIds":["unequal-access-intensifies-hiddenness"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"unequal-access-intensifies-hiddenness","component":"The practical response to missing revelation is still available through demanding moral action toward other people."}],"proposition":"A person without a vision still has a religiously supplied task: help others and live with demanding goodness rather than wait for mystical certainty.","inventoryRationale":"This provides the practical consequence of Ross's response to hiddenness."},{"moveId":"wrong-religion-may-be-worse","sectionId":"practice-pluralism-and-revelation","side":"con","speaker":"Alex O'Connor","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":2015,"endEvent":2035,"startMs":4052760,"endMs":4092160,"excerpt":"right? And that sounds great. It's like it feels like yeah, we've got a foot in the door, but at the same time, if I show up to to God in the afterlife and I say, \"Look, I'm I'm an agnostic. I sort of had a look around and I I couldn't really figure it out.\" You know, maybe he'll take some pity on me. But if I show up and say, \"No, no, no, I I worked it all out. I think that you I thought you didn't exist. I thought that there was some kind of weird pantheistic thing going on. I thought that electrons were made of consciousness.\" It feels like I'm I'm more inclined to actually have have like a false doctrine. Unless you have a worldview which specifically points towards the correct interpretation of God, you might sort of run yourself into into trouble, perhaps, no?"},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Alex's marked turn explicitly challenges the book's generic route into religion using pantheistic alternatives.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"con-pluralism-doctrine","tier":"subsidiary","polarity":"challenges"},"respondsToIds":["practice-before-complete-theology","hidden-person-called-to-virtue"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"practice-before-complete-theology","component":"Acting before resolving the nature of God may produce a confident but false doctrine rather than harmless religious approximation."},{"targetMoveId":"hidden-person-called-to-virtue","component":"Generic moral practice does not identify which metaphysical interpretation or divine object is correct."}],"proposition":"Generic evidence for mind or order may lead to pantheism rather than the true God, so premature religious confidence could be worse than honest agnosticism.","inventoryRationale":"This is the major pluralism objection to treating any religious step as directionally safe."},{"moveId":"practice-matters-more-than-conceptual-error","sectionId":"practice-pluralism-and-revelation","side":"pro","speaker":"Ross Douthat","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":2065,"endEvent":2083,"startMs":4155120,"endMs":4201000,"excerpt":"Your consciousness will continue into the next life. And what you do on Earth will shape what you experience in the next life and so on. Um and I think if you start out thinking like that, the fact that you conceive of God in the wrong way, yes, it it may still create a bunch of problems for you, right? That, you know, mean mean bad things for whatever happens to you in the next life. But it's still closer and directionally closer to the truth than just saying, \"I'm sitting here as an agnostic and you know, I I can't get anywhere. I can't I can't I can't get anywhere from here.\""},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Ross's marked answer directly distinguishes theological conception from the life produced by it.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"pro-practical-religion","tier":"central","polarity":"supports"},"respondsToIds":["wrong-religion-may-be-worse"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"wrong-religion-may-be-worse","component":"A mistaken conception remains directionally closer to truth when it produces communion, moral seriousness, and accountability rather than paralysis."}],"proposition":"A partly mistaken theology can remain directionally nearer truth than agnostic paralysis when it ties conduct to continued consciousness and future consequences.","inventoryRationale":"This is the central pro rebuttal to the danger of entering religion through an imperfect map."},{"moveId":"religions-share-starting-ground","sectionId":"practice-pluralism-and-revelation","side":"pro","speaker":"Ross Douthat","moveKind":"constructive","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":2107,"endEvent":2139,"startMs":4246200,"endMs":4312560,"excerpt":"there is a there is a there are things that are shared in common by the great world religions that taken together provide that in each case provided decent starting place for a human being interested in being in right relation to God and to their neighbor. >> Mhm. >> And then it also matters but then there are also fundamental differences between them that are in fact meaningful and do in fact make you know, some religions truer than others and you want to be in the truest possible religion. >> Yeah. >> But both of both of those things can be true at the same time. And it's just I think it's you know, the natural thing clearly for human beings is once you've stipulated that God is present in some way in all the major world religions, it's very natural to get to a kind of ecumenical shrug or a perennialist shrug where you say and therefore right it doesn't matter. But in but there's no logical reason why you can't say God is present in all the major world religions and it still matters tremendously which one you choose."},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Ross continues uninterrupted and states his explicit synthesis of commonality and difference among religions.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"pro-practical-religion","tier":"central","polarity":"supports"},"respondsToIds":[],"responseComponents":[],"proposition":"Major religions share enough truth to orient people toward God and neighbor, while their meaningful differences still make seeking the truest religion important.","inventoryRationale":"This is Ross's load-bearing reconciliation of religious inclusivism with non-equivalence."},{"moveId":"unequal-maps-create-dilemma","sectionId":"practice-pluralism-and-revelation","side":"con","speaker":"Alex O'Connor","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":2189,"endEvent":2224,"startMs":4411840,"endMs":4482920,"excerpt":">> Suppose but I I I think we have to sort of keep in mind that we're dealing with a a mapmaker who knows how to how to bring you to where you are supposed to be and who like without without whom you're guided into death and destruction. And this this mapmaker makes a really accurate map. Let Let's people sort of work it out for some time and then they roughly sort of figure out the terrain, but it's importantly mistaken in various ways. And so he sort of makes this perfect map and gives it to like some people and not everyone. When he could give it to everyone. And if that becomes a bit of a problem and people start thinking, \"I'm not really sure if I should trust this guy because like if this were really the way, why would he only give it to us?\" And then people sort of had a bit of a debate amongst themselves and the philosophers said, \"Well, I think ultimately that if you didn't have access to the proper map you can still find your way.\" And it'd be like, \"Well, then what's the what's the point in the in the map that we got then?\" It's a bit like what you"},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Alex's marked turn extends Ross's map analogy into a direct objection to selective revelation.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"con-pluralism-doctrine","tier":"subsidiary","polarity":"challenges"},"respondsToIds":["religions-share-starting-ground"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"religions-share-starting-ground","component":"An omniscient mapmaker's selective distribution of the accurate map is unexplained if erroneous maps risk destruction yet can also suffice."}],"proposition":"If God owns the accurate salvific map, giving it only to some is troubling; if inferior maps suffice, the special revelation's necessity becomes unclear.","inventoryRationale":"This is the strongest compact statement of the revelation-and-pluralism dilemma."},{"moveId":"hiddenness-enables-freedom-and-story","sectionId":"freedom-suffering-and-animal-pain","side":"pro","speaker":"Ross Douthat","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":2263,"endEvent":2295,"startMs":4548880,"endMs":4626320,"excerpt":"again, one, you have to take it as a given, I think, because it is the reality of the world that there is some kind of good that God achieves by not just settling everything from the start, right? Like, again, God is hidden from many people, right? I he and that and and that you know, that good has to be connected in some way or doesn't have to, but it's probably connected in some way to human freedom, right? That there is some kind of value in creaturely freedom. This is tends to be the Christian perspective, I think, for a good reason, right? Um but that if you have that, if you if you say, \"Okay, there is some good in a God who is not immediately present to his entire creation, one, and some kind of good in human beings having a set of free choices to make in that context, then you are in a situation where God can't just give everyone the map, and they can't all know it's the map immediately because then there's no you know, there's no actual freedom, right? There's no actual sort of dynamic journeying out and returning. But that"},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Ross's marked response addresses why God does not settle revelation universally from the start.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"pro-hiddenness-freedom","tier":"subsidiary","polarity":"supports"},"respondsToIds":["unequal-maps-create-dilemma"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"unequal-maps-create-dilemma","component":"Universal unmistakable revelation would remove the freedom, journey, and differentiated roles that may constitute goods within creation."}],"proposition":"God's not settling everything immediately may preserve creaturely freedom and a dynamic journey whose value requires that everyone not receive an unmistakable map.","inventoryRationale":"This is Ross's main theodicy of selective revelation and hiddenness."},{"moveId":"animal-suffering-presses-triomni-god","sectionId":"freedom-suffering-and-animal-pain","side":"con","speaker":"Alex O'Connor","moveKind":"constructive","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":2413,"endEvent":2443,"startMs":4882000,"endMs":4939240,"excerpt":">> I don't think there are really many good reasons to to be a positive A atheist. I think there are good holes to pick in in the case for and I think there are good holes to pick in particular religious manifestations. So, I think the problem of evil is a is a huge problem against any kind of so to speak tri-omni God, you know, omnibenevolent and omnipotent and omniscient. I think it does pose a classical problem. The strongest version is the problem of animal suffering. The fact that it's not just humans, but non-human animals who don't seem to morally develop in the same way, who don't seem to have free will, who don't seem to or if they do, it seems to not really matter that much, who probably aren't compensated in some kind of afterlife. And if they are, it seems to be a kind of crude Bullingdon Club esque, we'll we'll trash the room and then throw some money and you better be grateful cuz we're paying you what it's worth kind of thing. It doesn't seem quite right. Um that that does have a problem that does pose a problem for me for for an omnipotent omniscient omnibenevolent God, but then"},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Alex answers Ross's question about his principal objection and explicitly presents animal suffering as its strongest form.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"con-hiddenness-evil","tier":"central","polarity":"challenges"},"respondsToIds":[],"responseComponents":[],"proposition":"Animal suffering strongly challenges an omniscient, omnipotent, and omnibenevolent God because animals lack the usual defenses of moral growth, freedom, or assured compensation.","inventoryRationale":"This introduces the debate's principal positive evidential challenge to a specific conception of God."},{"moveId":"animal-experience-remains-uncertain","sectionId":"freedom-suffering-and-animal-pain","side":"pro","speaker":"Ross Douthat","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":2463,"endEvent":2490,"startMs":4967880,"endMs":5023320,"excerpt":">> we have so little access, right, to animal consciousness to know, I mean, I'll you know, I I read >> Yeah. >> people who get very into the idea that, you know, there's absolutely nothing worse than the life of a wild animal. >> Yeah, right. >> Because it lives right, it lives and and you can say, you know, you you can say maybe that's true, right? That like the you live under constant threat. You're just in fight or flight mode all the time. It's just like pain and sorrow and misery and then it ends with death at a predator's hands, right? But But that is a you know, extrapolation or imposition of human concepts and the idea that the the possibility that you know, animals wild animals in nature are actually whatever their happiness is are in fact happy in the context of a world that can still end with their violent death. I don't think that can be excluded at all. Now, what we do to animals maybe is a separate question, right?"},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Ross's marked response directly disputes assumptions about the conscious quality of wild-animal lives.","importance":2,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"pro-suffering-theosis","tier":"subsidiary","polarity":"supports"},"respondsToIds":["animal-suffering-presses-triomni-god"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"animal-suffering-presses-triomni-god","component":"Human observers lack enough access to animal consciousness to infer that wild lives are dominated by misery rather than containing fitting happiness."}],"proposition":"We know too little about animal consciousness to exclude that wild animals live fittingly happy lives despite threat and eventual violent death.","inventoryRationale":"This is Ross's first direct evidential limitation on the animal-suffering argument."},{"moveId":"moral-intuition-cuts-against-theism","sectionId":"freedom-suffering-and-animal-pain","side":"con","speaker":"Alex O'Connor","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":2502,"endEvent":2534,"startMs":5040600,"endMs":5103640,"excerpt":"I get what you mean. You can sort of recourse into a kind of into a kind of we can't know a kind of um uh sort of theodicy a bit a bit bit like Job's. It's like, well, who who are you? Who is this that that darkens counsel by words without knowledge? You know, who who are you to know? That's fair enough. You you can say that, but it it seems like in other contexts religious people want me to rely upon my moral intuitions. They want me to rely upon them to prove the existence of God. They say, \"Well, look, you know that certain things are right and wrong. >> Mhm. >> You know that there's a way that the world should be. And I can't prove that to you, but that intuition is so strong that you should trust it. And if that's the case, it must be grounded in God.\" And I say, \"Well, one of my intuitions is that if a single one wild animal experiences gratuitous suffering that isn't necessary, that isn't justified, if that happens on one occasion at any point in the billion-year history of life on this earth, that that at the very least counts against the plausibility of an omnibenevolent creator."},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Alex's marked response contrasts the skeptical theodicy with religious reliance on moral intuitions.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"con-hiddenness-evil","tier":"central","polarity":"challenges"},"respondsToIds":["animal-experience-remains-uncertain"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"animal-experience-remains-uncertain","component":"The same moral intuitions used in arguments for God make even one instance of gratuitous animal suffering evidence against an omnibenevolent creator."}],"proposition":"Religious appeals to moral intuition cannot be discarded here: one genuinely gratuitous episode of animal suffering would count against an omnibenevolent creator.","inventoryRationale":"This is the key consistency objection to answering animal pain with broad skepticism."},{"moveId":"mysterious-residue-expected-under-freedom","sectionId":"freedom-suffering-and-animal-pain","side":"pro","speaker":"Ross Douthat","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":2601,"endEvent":2635,"startMs":5200880,"endMs":5269760,"excerpt":">> I I think that's I think that's certainly what you have to say if you believe in the a benevolent God. Yes. And I think that's And I think there is you do end up saying that, you know, we can account for, yeah, you know, the non-gratuity You can account for the non-gratuity of the existence of suffering, and you can account for the non-gratuity of lots of forms of suffering. But yes, there's still a mysterious residua that rests on, you know, it's the the Dostoevsky example, you know, it's like the baby's tossed on spear points, right? Or something like that. And and yes, there there again. But this is sort of also what you would to just to sort of talk my own book again, right? Not this book, but like the larger argument, right? That is what you what you would expect in a universe set up with God partially hidden, not fully hidden, but like not available at all times, right? Or available at all times, but not perceived to be available at all times, where human beings are you know, perceive themselves to be set free and to act freely, you would expect there to be some things that we can't account for."},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Ross's marked response concedes a residue and relates it to his previously stated hiddenness framework.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"pro-suffering-theosis","tier":"subsidiary","polarity":"supports"},"respondsToIds":["animal-suffering-presses-triomni-god","moral-intuition-cuts-against-theism"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"animal-suffering-presses-triomni-god","component":"Many forms of suffering can be rendered non-gratuitous, while an unexplained residue is unsurprising in a partially hidden, freedom-permitting world."},{"targetMoveId":"moral-intuition-cuts-against-theism","component":"The admitted intuitive residue does not establish gratuity when the larger purposes remain inaccessible."}],"proposition":"A benevolent-God view must deny gratuitous suffering, but a mysterious residue is expected in a world where God is partly hidden and creatures experience freedom.","inventoryRationale":"This is Ross's qualified synthesis rather than an attempt to deny every appearance of pointless suffering."},{"moveId":"animal-pain-may-be-more-intense","sectionId":"freedom-suffering-and-animal-pain","side":"con","speaker":"Alex O'Connor","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":2653,"endEvent":2687,"startMs":5296680,"endMs":5357880,"excerpt":"mean, you you also hinted at the idea that we kind of anthropomorphize, that we project human like experiences onto animals. We don't know what it's like for them to suffer. I think that cuts both ways though. I mean, it could be the case that animals just kind of don't really feel pain in the way that we do. That's what C.S. Lewis suggested. That's what William Lane Craig has suggested in the past. They seem to have backtracked recently on that. Um but it goes the other way too. I think that dogs experience smell far more acutely than we could possibly imagine. I think that hawks have much better eyesight than we do. Like they experience eyesight more precisely and more acutely than we could imagine. And why is that? Well, because they rely on those senses more than we do. And I feel like wild animals rely on their crude sensory pain receptors much more than we do too with our reason. We don't need them as much. I think there's a good evolutionary reason. If we're going to accept that we should take seriously this this possible experiential gap between us and animals, there's a better argument to make that those animals experience more pain, more acutely, more intensely than we do. And then well, you have have even more of a problem with that."},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Alex continues his marked rebuttal and uses evolutionary sensory comparisons to reverse Ross's uncertainty claim.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"con-hiddenness-evil","tier":"central","polarity":"challenges"},"respondsToIds":["animal-experience-remains-uncertain","mysterious-residue-expected-under-freedom"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"animal-experience-remains-uncertain","component":"Uncertainty cuts both ways because evolutionary reliance on pain may make animal suffering more acute rather than less conscious."},{"targetMoveId":"mysterious-residue-expected-under-freedom","component":"The immense number and possible intensity of animal pains make universal inclusion within a divine plan especially implausible."}],"proposition":"The animal-consciousness gap cuts both ways: evolutionary reliance on raw sensation may make wild-animal pain more acute, intensifying rather than dissolving the problem.","inventoryRationale":"This is Alex's strongest reply to the epistemic uncertainty surrounding animal consciousness."},{"moveId":"heaven-shows-suffering-not-required","sectionId":"heaven-theosis-and-final-synthesis","side":"con","speaker":"Alex O'Connor","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":2762,"endEvent":2781,"startMs":5504440,"endMs":5546320,"excerpt":">> You certainly can say that, but then I I like to ask people, um you know, um do you think that free will exists in in heaven in the afterlife? Do you think that the afterlife is a life that's worth having? And if the answer is yes to both, then you either have to accept that it is possible for someone to commit a holocaust in heaven. Which seems ludicrous. Or you have to suggest that actually there can be a state of existence in which people are free and happy and living in a way that's worth living that does not necessitate the infliction of of suffering and the the experience of suffering. You know what I mean? You know what I mean?"},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Alex's marked turn directly tests Ross's claim that suffering may be required for a worthwhile free world.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"con-freedom-heaven","tier":"subsidiary","polarity":"challenges"},"respondsToIds":["mysterious-residue-expected-under-freedom"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"mysterious-residue-expected-under-freedom","component":"If heaven contains freedom and worthwhile happiness without atrocities, neither freedom nor worthwhile existence generally requires suffering."}],"proposition":"If heaven is both free and worth living in, yet excludes atrocities, then freedom and valuable existence can occur without suffering.","inventoryRationale":"This is the central internal challenge to the freedom-and-soul-making defense."},{"moveId":"earthly-progression-has-distinct-value","sectionId":"heaven-theosis-and-final-synthesis","side":"pro","speaker":"Ross Douthat","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":2787,"endEvent":2804,"startMs":5553320,"endMs":5592560,"excerpt":"it is there's a world in which having creatures who can advance to holiness from a creaturely status. >> Mhm. >> That that world is good in and of itself to a degree that justifies the existence of suffering, right? Which is But but it is the you have to say that like that progression is valuable, right? And again, this goes back to the point I was making like there has to be some value in this experience. It has to be the case that the perfected soul in heaven has something that an angel does not have."},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Ross's marked response directly distinguishes perfected creatures from beings created without earthly formation.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"pro-suffering-theosis","tier":"subsidiary","polarity":"supports"},"respondsToIds":["heaven-shows-suffering-not-required"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"heaven-shows-suffering-not-required","component":"A suffering-free heaven does not replace the distinct good of creatures freely progressing from earthly limitation to holiness."}],"proposition":"Earthly life is justified if creaturely advancement toward holiness has a distinct good, so a perfected human soul may possess something an untested angel lacks.","inventoryRationale":"This is Ross's main answer to the heaven counterexample."},{"moveId":"human-violence-fixes-eternal-rank","sectionId":"heaven-theosis-and-final-synthesis","side":"con","speaker":"Alex O'Connor","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":2923,"endEvent":2953,"startMs":5818920,"endMs":5876120,"excerpt":">> But you know, if if I say like if I say, you know, why would God allow me to go and harm a bunch of people in this world? You might say something like, well, because in the afterlife they're going to be elevated and you're not. And that sort of makes up for it. But if I said to you like, well, why is it that I'm allowed to go and like you know, kill a pregnant woman and send her unborn child into the afterlife such that not only in this world that they then get glorified in heaven, but that the mother gets to go to a higher plane of of heaven and her aborted like child >> Well, I okay. Here here here >> is lowered. And that's up to me? Like I get to determine their like eternal like hierarchical state in heaven with God? Such that if I hadn't made that free decision her and her child would experience bliss together in the same hierarchy one day, but because I've made that decision, nothing to do with God cuz of course I have libertarian free will. That child is now going to be on the lowest rung of heaven. You know, that it just seems so so implausible."},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Alex's marked turn develops a concrete implication of differing heavenly outcomes after Ross's theosis defense.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"con-freedom-heaven","tier":"subsidiary","polarity":"challenges"},"respondsToIds":["earthly-progression-has-distinct-value"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"earthly-progression-has-distinct-value","component":"If earthly development determines heavenly fulfillment, one person's violence can permanently deprive a victim of the development God intended."}],"proposition":"A murderer could determine an unborn victim's eternal level by preventing earthly development, an implausible consequence of tying heavenly fulfillment to lived trials.","inventoryRationale":"This is a major downstream objection to the proposed special value of earthly formation."},{"moveId":"divine-love-not-oppressive-hierarchy","sectionId":"heaven-theosis-and-final-synthesis","side":"pro","speaker":"Ross Douthat","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":2954,"endEvent":2989,"startMs":5874080,"endMs":5954200,"excerpt":">> again here it's important not and and this is, you know, that this sounds like one of these things that religious people do, right? Where they're but you're not you're not supposed to see it as this kind of like the metaphor of the ladder >> Yeah, sure. >> implies like a kind that you're suffering, right? From being lower down, right? And it's more like again to take the sort of prime example. It's like God loves the Virgin Mary in a special way. He loves maybe the pregnant mother who you've done something terrible to and who achieves, you know, who has some kind of experience because of it. He loves them in a in a special way that doesn't reduce the experience of being loved by God that that unborn child feels or that you would feel if if you yourself repent, right? Um and again, I don't, you know, this is not like this is not a a provable Yeah. A provable thing, but it's I I don't think it's impossible to conceptualize, right? But like like like the, you know, the the basic claim, right? Again, is that sort of God's love is like human love, but different from human love, above human love and so on and I don't I don't think it's ridiculous to say that like in the afterlife you could be content with God's love"},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Ross's marked answer directly rejects the ladder-like interpretation of heavenly difference.","importance":2,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"pro-suffering-theosis","tier":"subsidiary","polarity":"supports"},"respondsToIds":["human-violence-fixes-eternal-rank"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"human-violence-fixes-eternal-rank","component":"Different modes of divine love need not produce deprivation, resentment, oppression, or diminished contentment for the victim."}],"proposition":"Differences in heavenly fulfillment need not be oppressive ranks, because every person can be fully content in divine love even when others receive it differently.","inventoryRationale":"This is Ross's direct attempt to remove the alleged harm from unequal heavenly outcomes."},{"moveId":"freedom-can-remove-gods-intended-life","sectionId":"heaven-theosis-and-final-synthesis","side":"con","speaker":"Alex O'Connor","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":3057,"endEvent":3088,"startMs":6084360,"endMs":6148360,"excerpt":">> I definitely want to be very careful not to say something like when these people are in heaven, whatever it is that the mother gets that the child doesn't, uh will be experienced as as better, will be preferred, there'll be any resentment or comparison. Totally not. I have I have no problem saying that they are they're both just living the best life, totally chill, having a great time. However, if the plan for that child had been by God that they would become an adult, and that plan was corrupted by someone who freely chose to go and kill that child. Well, clearly the the sort of ideal situation, the thing they were commanded to do was to not do that. >> Mhm. >> And the fact that we're not just minds floating around, but we have been embodied for some kind of purpose, that then there must be something better or more preferable to God about us going through that experience. And it seems troublesome that I'm given the power in this worldview to take that away from somebody eternally."},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Alex's marked continuation carefully grants heavenly contentment before isolating the remaining deprivation claim.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"con-freedom-heaven","tier":"subsidiary","polarity":"challenges"},"respondsToIds":["divine-love-not-oppressive-hierarchy","earthly-progression-has-distinct-value"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"divine-love-not-oppressive-hierarchy","component":"Absence of resentment does not erase the fact that another agent prevented an embodied life God preferred for the victim."},{"targetMoveId":"earthly-progression-has-distinct-value","component":"If embodiment has a divine purpose, libertarian freedom appears able to remove that intended good from someone forever."}],"proposition":"Even without heavenly resentment, embodiment must serve a preferred purpose, so it remains troubling that another person's free choice can remove that good eternally.","inventoryRationale":"This preserves Alex's objection after granting Ross's account of contentment and non-comparative love."},{"moveId":"freedom-and-glorification-constrain-world","sectionId":"heaven-theosis-and-final-synthesis","side":"pro","speaker":"Ross Douthat","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":3099,"endEvent":3125,"startMs":6167720,"endMs":6230000,"excerpt":"there are things that are just somewhat contradictory. And you it's very hard to construct a world in which you have the freedom that you need to have the experiences that you need to achieve this particular kind of theosis and and so on. And also in that world, you can never harm anyone else, you know, you can never affect anyone else's place in eternity through through your actions, right? Um but you do have to conceive, right, of God as a storyteller who does, in the end um you know, he's he's telling a story that works with your free will and brings about >> Mhm. >> an outcome that is not the only possible outcome, right? Like to you know, to take the There's There's many different ways that you could write a Dickens novel or Lord of the Rings or anything else and get to a happy ending. And characters could have a lot of different fates and so on. Um right? But God is delivering a version of the story."},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Ross's marked response directly invokes possible-world constraints, freedom, theosis, and the storyteller model.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"pro-suffering-theosis","tier":"subsidiary","polarity":"supports"},"respondsToIds":["freedom-can-remove-gods-intended-life"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"freedom-can-remove-gods-intended-life","component":"The freedom required for transformative experience may be inseparable from real power to affect others, while providence still brings the story to a good outcome."}],"proposition":"A world enabling freedom and theosis may necessarily permit people to affect others' destinies, while God works with those choices toward one good possible ending.","inventoryRationale":"This is the final developed pro response to the freedom-and-eternal-harm problem."},{"moveId":"evil-targets-specific-religions","sectionId":"heaven-theosis-and-final-synthesis","side":"con","speaker":"Alex O'Connor","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":3175,"endEvent":3206,"startMs":6329000,"endMs":6391200,"excerpt":">> That's why I answered your question very carefully earlier when you were like, is this you you seem to be asking like is this the biggest objection you have to and I'm like it depends on what you mean. If you mean, is this my biggest objection to the view that there is some kind of creative agency behind the universe and the answer is just no because you can account for it. But it is my biggest objection to particular presentations because sometimes the way in which you can account for it is by moving away from a God who has in individual instantiations of people who go to heaven in an afterlife because Jesus died on the cross for them to something like, well, to keep God moral we seem to imply that the at least the plausibility of reincarnation, maybe the existence of one self which is sort of an illusion in so far as it's distinguished sort of distinguished amongst different kinds of people and that actually heaven doesn't really work in the way that we thought and I'm totally I I agree with you that if again if if God tells me actually the way it works is like this and like that I would just be like cool, awesome. That's that's really great."},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Alex explicitly returns to and carefully qualifies his earlier answer about the scope of his objection.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"con-no-obligation","tier":"motion","polarity":"challenges"},"respondsToIds":["freedom-and-glorification-constrain-world"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"freedom-and-glorification-constrain-world","component":"Speculative alternatives can preserve a generic creator only by moving away from the particular Christian account under defense."}],"proposition":"Suffering is not decisive against every creative agency, but proposed solutions often preserve divine morality only by abandoning particular Christian doctrines for heterodox alternatives.","inventoryRationale":"This is Alex's final synthesis limiting his conclusion while retaining the skeptical burden against specific religion."},{"moveId":"ordinary-practice-bears-fruit","sectionId":"heaven-theosis-and-final-synthesis","side":"pro","speaker":"Ross Douthat","moveKind":"reply","sourceSpan":{"startEvent":3219,"endEvent":3256,"startMs":6408880,"endMs":6499840,"excerpt":"you are going to church on Sunday. You are praying. You're trying to talk to God, whoever God may be. Right? And you are trying to live up to the model of what God wants that is offered by the person of his son who lived a pretty intense life on earth, made a lot of demands of people, suffered a great deal. Right? Um there is a way in which like under lots of different theological schema. Just that everyday life should be enough, right? Like you you don't like let's say you have your your you know you're a church and you have a heretical thought. You're like, \"Actually, the Christian worldview would make much more sense if we imported this concept from Buddhism, right?\" And like okay, maybe you're a professional theologian or an academic and there's something interesting that you can do with that. Or maybe you're a really intense mystic and you're going to have this experience, right? But most likely that's a distraction. Right? And the the kind of the everydayness of the practice of religion bears its own fruits that you know, are just going to be there waiting for you, right? And don't depend on you settling the question of do we need 10% more reincarnation to make God's system work?"},"quoteEligibleExactSpans":[],"attributionConfidence":"high","attributionBasis":"Ross's final substantive marked turn precedes the closing thanks and summarizes the practical case for religion.","importance":3,"burdenContact":{"bridgeId":"pro-obligation","tier":"motion","polarity":"supports"},"respondsToIds":["evil-targets-specific-religions"],"responseComponents":[{"targetMoveId":"evil-targets-specific-religions","component":"Unresolved heterodox possibilities need not suspend ordinary prayer, worship, and imitation of a demanding religious moral model."}],"proposition":"Ordinary worship, prayer, and moral imitation bear fruits across theological schemes, so speculative efforts to perfect the system should not displace religious practice.","inventoryRationale":"This is the final synthesis reconnecting unresolved objections to the original claim of practical religious obligation."}],"selectionBalanceAudit":{"thresholds":{"totalAbsoluteDifference":3,"sectionAbsoluteDifference":2},"totals":{"pro":21,"con":17,"absoluteDifference":4},"sections":[{"sectionId":"obligation-and-consciousness","pro":4,"con":2,"absoluteDifference":2},{"sectionId":"fine-tuning-and-cosmic-order","pro":4,"con":3,"absoluteDifference":1},{"sectionId":"experience-hiddenness-and-atheism","pro":2,"con":2,"absoluteDifference":0},{"sectionId":"practice-pluralism-and-revelation","pro":4,"con":3,"absoluteDifference":1},{"sectionId":"freedom-suffering-and-animal-pain","pro":3,"con":3,"absoluteDifference":0},{"sectionId":"heaven-theosis-and-final-synthesis","pro":4,"con":4,"absoluteDifference":0}],"rationaleRequired":true,"rationale":"Douthat advances four more distinct selected moves because he bears the affirmative book-length obligation case and supplies separate constructives on consciousness, practice, hiddenness, and theosis; O'Connor's challenges are often longer integrated replies. Every asymmetry preserves a non-mergeable inference actually present in the source rather than padding the skeptical side or deleting affirmative burdens."},"publicationCapacityAudit":{"maximumSelectedMovesPerSidePerSection":4,"sections":[{"sectionId":"obligation-